		"nodes": {
			"1": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "MAP\n\nThe Map node scales the input from one range to another. For example, mapping 10 from the range (0,100) tot the range(50, 60) results in 51. The skew attribute can be used to skew the scaling to the beginning or ending of the scale. With the skew set to 0.5 the scale will be evenly distributed.\n\n- Patch Explained -\n\n• The Sine produces a value running from -0.5 to 0.5 and back.\n• This value modulates the Y position of the yellow circle.\n•.Both of the Map nodes map the incoming value to a new range of -0.2 to 0.2\n• The blue circle is modulated by the top Map node that has a normal skew of 0.5\n• The red circle is modulated by the lower Map that that has it’s skew set to 0.2\n• The lower skew divides the values unevenly across the range (in this case more to the lower end). This results in the red circle hanging in the top longer before accelerating downwards. Notice that the blue and red circle reach the bottom at the same time.\n"
					}
				},
